section.section_3_3
    h2 Stalin Before Stalinism
    p
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |life story has always been more than biography. There is wonder at the achievement — the son of a Georgian cobbler ascending the heights of world power; the architect of an
        span(data-type="event" data-id="InSU") industrial revolution
        |and the destruction of millions of the people he ruled; the leader of the state that stopped the bloody expansion of fascism.
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |story is that of the making of the
        span(data-type="place" data-id="USSR") Soviet Union
        |and a particular vision of what he called socialism. His biographers have often placed historical imagination at the service of a specific politics of eroding (or lauding) the Stalinist inheritance.
    p
        |Such a life story cannot be separated from an evaluation of that life’s work. In twenty-first-century
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plRu") Russia
        |, visions of
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |are deployed to justify yet another slide into authoritarianism, while in the West, the entirety of the Soviet experience is often reduced to
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |and Stalinism, the
        span(data-type="event" data-id="StGP") Great Purges
        |, and the
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="Gulg") gulag
        |. The drama of his life, the achievements and tragedies, are so morally and emotionally charged that they challenge the usual practices of historical objectivity and scholarly neutrality. When it comes to
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |, gossip is reported as fact; legend provides meaning.
    p
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s
        |early years — before he was
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |— are the most elusive and obscure period of his life. Here the lasting fascination with the demon dictator is matched by an irresistible temptation to make his childhood and youth “useful,” by investing them with the first signs of the paranoid revolutionary-from-above of the
        span(data-type="date") 1930s
        |, the arch-criminal who presided over the death of millions.
    p
        |Those who “know” the autocratic
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |of totalitarian
        span(data-type="place" data-id="plRu") Russia
        |have read back the characteristics of the general secretary into the young
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |, emphasizing what fits — violence, paranoia, arrogance, and the need to dominate — and rejecting what does not — romanticism, literary sensibility, love for his homeland, and revolutionary idealism. As important as
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin’s early life was to the formation of his personality, the difficulties of reconstructing it from the few extant memoirs about his youth and scanty available documentation have sometimes led to flimsily-built psychoanalytic speculation, and, at other times, to fanciful arguments that
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |must have been an agent for the tsar’s
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="Okhr") Okhrana
        |(political police).